<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · c. 1170 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Can vei la lauzeta mover</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A love song from the 1100s by a troubadour, a poet-singer of the Middle Ages, who wishes he were as happy as a lark.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bernart de Ventadorn</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Bernart de Ventadorn</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the troubadours of Occitania</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>12th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and melody by the troubadour Bernart de Ventadorn (12th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vei-la-lauzeta-mover">Can vei la lauzeta mover</a>&quot; is a song in the Occitan language by Bernart de Ventadorn, a troubadour of the 12th century. It is among the oldest and the best known of all troubadour songs. Both its words and its melody survive, in variants from three different <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Its lauzeta is a lark.</p><p>The song is a first-person poem of seven stanzas of eight lines each, with a closing coda of four lines. It has been called one of the first poems to dramatise the effect of someone actually speaking in the present.</p><p>It travelled beyond the Occitan-speaking south. In the 13th century it was one of three Occitan songs set into the French romance Guillaume de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a>, and it is one of two similar insertions in Gerbert de Montreuil's Le roman de la violette. Some scholars have suggested that it inspired a tercet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a> (Paradiso XX:73–75), which also describes a lark in flight; others think Dante may have met the image through the poet Bondie Dietaiuti, or that a lark's flight was too common a sight for any link to be drawn. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> Pound translated parts of the song in Canto 6 of The Cantos and returned to its image in Canto 117.</p><p>The song is still performed, and it has been recorded on many albums.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of unrequited love. The singer watches a lark rise joyfully towards the sun and fall, and wishes he could feel such joy, but his love for a woman who does not return it keeps him from it. He compares himself to figures of legend who were undone by love and resolves to go into exile if she will not love him. The mood is longing and bitter.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Occitan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Occitan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Can vei la lauzeta mover</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When I see the lark beat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">De joi sas alas contral rai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its wings for joy against the sunbeam,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Que s’oblid’ e·s laissa chazer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that it forgets itself and lets itself fall</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Per la doussor c’al cor li vai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the sweetness that enters its heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ai! Tan grans enveya m’en ve</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, such great envy comes over me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">De cui qu’eu veya jauzion,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of anyone I see rejoicing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Meravilhas ai, car desse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I marvel that at once</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Lo cor de dezirer no·m fon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my heart does not melt from desire.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ai, las! tan cuidava saber</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Alas, I thought I knew so much</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">D’amor, e tan petit en sai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of love, and I know so little,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Car eu d’amar no·m posc tener</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for I cannot keep from loving</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Celeis don ja pro non aurai.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>her from whom I shall never have any good.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Tout m’a mo cor, e tout m’a me,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She has taken my heart, and taken me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E se mezeis e tot lo mon;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and herself and all the world;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E can se·m tolc, no·m laisset re</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and when she took herself from me, she left me nothing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Mas dezirer e cor volon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but desire and a longing heart.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 60 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Anc non agui de me poder</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have never had power over myself</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ni no fui meus de l’or’ en sai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nor been my own from the hour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Que·m laisset en sos olhs vezer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she let me look into her eyes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">En un miralh que mout me plai.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>into a mirror that pleases me greatly.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Miralhs, pus me mirei en te,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mirror, since I saw myself in you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">M’an mort li sospir de preon,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sighs from the depths have killed me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">C’aissi·m perdei com perdet se</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for I lost myself just as</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Lo bels Narcisus en la fon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>fair Narcissus lost himself in the fountain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">De las domnas me dezesper;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I despair of ladies;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ja mais en lor no·m fiarai;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>never again will I trust in them;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">C’aissi com las solh chaptener,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and just as I used to uphold them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Enaissi las deschaptenrai.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so now I shall abandon them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Pois vei c’una pro no m’en te</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since I see that not one takes my part</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Vas leis que·m destrui e·m cofon,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>against her who destroys and confounds me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Totas las dopt’ e las mescre,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I fear and distrust them all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Car be sai c’atretals se son.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for I know well that they are all alike.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">D’aisso·s fa be femna parer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this my lady shows herself</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ma domna, per qu’e·lh’ o retrai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a woman indeed, and I reproach her for it:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Car no vol so c’om deu voler,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for she does not want what one ought to want,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E so c’om li deveda, fai.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and what one forbids her, she does.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Chazutz sui en mala merce,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have fallen out of favour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Et ai be faih co·l fols en pon;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and have done just like the fool on the bridge;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E no sai per que m’esdeve,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and I do not know why this befalls me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Mas car trop puyei contra mon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>unless because I climbed too high up the mountain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Merces es perduda, per ver,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mercy is lost, in truth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">(Et eu non o saubi anc mai),</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>(and I never knew it till now),</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Car cilh qui plus en degr’aver,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for she who ought to have the most of it</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">No·n a ges, et on la querrai ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>has none at all, and where shall I seek it?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">A ! Can mal sembla, qui la ve,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, how little would anyone who sees her think</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Qued aquest chaitiu deziron</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that this wretched, longing man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Que ja ses leis non aura be,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who will never have any good without her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Laisse morrir, que no l’aon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she would let die, and not help him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Pus ab midons no·m pot valer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since with my lady neither prayers nor pity</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Precs ni merces ni·l dreihz qu’eu ai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nor the right I have can help me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Ni a leis no ven a plazer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and it gives her no pleasure</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Qu’eu l’am, ja mais no·lh o dirai.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that I love her, I shall never tell her so again.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Aissi·m part de leis e·m recre;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So I part from her and give up;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Mort m’a, e per mort li respon,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she has killed me, and as a dead man I answer her,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E vau m’en, pus ilh no·m rete,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and I go away, since she does not keep me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Chaitius, en issilh, no sai on.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>wretched, into exile, I know not where.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Tristans, ges no·n auretz de me,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tristan, you will have nothing more from me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">Qu’eu m’en vau, chaitius, no sai on.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for I go away, wretched, I know not where.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">De chantar me gic e·m recre,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I give up singing and renounce it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="oc">E de joi e d’amor m’escon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and I hide myself from joy and love.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Bernart de Ventadorn; the text established by Carl Appel (1915). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Can_vei_la_lauzeta_mover" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>The English of the first stanzas follows the site owner's own translation; the rest is the Institute's, line by line.</li><li>line 5.6: &quot;the fool on the bridge&quot;, a proverb: the fool who rides on across a bridge instead of dismounting, and falls.</li><li>line 8.1: Tristan is a code name (senhal), probably for the lady or a friend.</li><li>Appel's raised dot (no·m, e·s) marks a word joined to the one before it.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
